+## 5. Bit-exactness (qemu-ppc, AltiVec vs C)
+
+Setup:
+
+- The **same patched source** is built twice for linux-ppc32 BE with gcc 13.3. The AltiVec build uses `--enable-altivec -DXCLOUD360_VMX_SAFE=1`; the C build uses `--disable-altivec`.
+- Both use `--cpu=7450 --disable-runtime-cpudetect` and pthreads. Scripts: `xcloud360/cfg-qemu.sh`.
+- They run under `qemu-ppc-static -cpu 7450`.
+- The gcc-13 AltiVec library also contains **0** unsupported instructions (`results/vmxaudit-qemu-av.txt`), so qemu ran exactly the legal-subset vector algorithms.
+- Harness: `xcloud360/harness.c`. It uses av_parser_parse2 and avcodec_decode_video2, the same flags as the app (CODEC_FLAG2_FAST), and computes an MD5 per frame over the visible Y, U and V planes. The thread modes are thread_count 1, and 3 with FF_THREAD_FRAME.
+- Independent reference: x86 `ffmpeg -f framemd5` (system ffmpeg), which hashes the same packed yuv420p bytes.
+
+| Stream | Frames | AltiVec t1 | AltiVec t3 | C t1 | C t3 | vs x86 ref |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| GOLDEN-synth-720p60-baseline, first 300 pictures (CBP, CAVLC) | 300 | = | = | = | = | all identical |
+| GOLDEN-synth-720p60-baseline-5s (the embedded stream, CBP) | 120 | = | = | = | = | all identical |
+| main-cabac-bf0 (x264 Main, CABAC, weightp=2, testsrc2 720p60 5 s) | 300 | = | = | = | = | all identical |
+| main-cabac-bf3 (Main, CABAC, 3 B-frames, weightb, weightp=2) | 300 | = | = | = | = | all identical |
+
+**All 16 runs give the same per-frame MD5s, with 0 decode errors.** Extra runs also match: parser fed in 4 KB chunks, `FLAG2_FAST` off, and `LOW_DELAY` with 1 thread.
+
+Mutation check (`xcloud360/mutate.sh`): breaking each rewritten routine on purpose is caught at frames 1-3. The broken routines were the chroma bias, qpel h ×20, hv stage-2 shift, weight and biweight, plus chroma on the CBP golden stream. So these paths really run, and the harness detects errors in them.
+
+The two Main test streams are in `xcloud360/streams/`. They were generated with `ffmpeg -f lavfi -i testsrc2=size=1280x720:rate=60 -t 5 -c:v libx264 -profile:v main -bf 0|3 -g 120 -b:v 4500k`.
+
+## 6. The "decode error around frame ~117 with frame threading"
+
+**It does not reproduce as a decode error.** The app's exact loop (`harness ... app`, stop on error, no flush) on GOLDEN-synth-720p60-baseline-5s gives:
+
+- **thread_count 3 (frame):** 119 packets, **117 frames output**, 0 errors.
+- **thread_count 1:** 119 packets, 119 frames output, 0 errors.
+
+This holds for both C and AltiVec, and the output equals the reference prefix.
+
+Cause:
+
+- The embedded stream has only **120 pictures**, with an IDR every 30 frames. It is not 5 s at 60 fps.
+- The app's embedded loop ends with `if (left == 0) break;`. It never flushes the parser, which still holds the last access unit, so only 119 packets reach the decoder.
+- It also never drains the decoder (`avpkt size 0`), so with frame threading the thread_count−1 = 2 frames still in flight are lost.
+- 120 − 1 − 2 = **117**.
+- The decode-error path and the EOF path both do just `break`, with no message, so an "early stop" at 117 looks the same as an error.
+
+Fix: `xcloud360/app-embedded-eof-drain.patch`, against app/h264play/source/main.c 0.14.4-split. **It has not been applied to the repo.** It flushes the parser with an empty buffer at EOF, drains the decoder with NULL packets until `got == 0`, and prints whether the loop ended at EOF or on a decode error. It compiles and links, and the harness's `full` mode, which uses the same flush and drain pattern, outputs all 120 frames with 1 and 3 threads. If the console ever shows a *real* decode error there, the remaining suspect is deps/xenon-pthread, which qemu does not exercise because it uses glibc pthreads.
+
+## 7. What was and was not verified
+
+Verified:
+
+- The Xenon build compiles cleanly. The only warnings are pre-existing ones (cast-qual, unused vdst in the put variants, unused dct_quantize).
+- libavcodec.a and libavutil.a are produced.
+- The opcode audit finds 0 unsupported instructions, with two independent methods and a positive control.
+- app/h264play links against the tree.
+- Under qemu-ppc (classic AltiVec semantics, big-endian), the AltiVec and C builds are bit-exact with each other and with x86 ffmpeg, on CBP/CAVLC and Main/CABAC streams (with and without B-frames and weighted prediction), at 1 and 3 frame threads.
+
+**Not verified:**
+
+- **No run on Xenon hardware.**
+- **No performance measurement.** qemu timings mean nothing for this, and the float chroma/weight paths and the shift-based multiplies cost a few more instructions than the original vmladduhm code. The real speed-up must come from the console STAT telemetry (dec_ms, dec_fps).
+- Xenon-specific behaviour qemu cannot model: VMX128 float rounding and denormals. These cannot matter here, because every float value is an exact integer below 2^24.
+- Stack alignment of the decode threads was checked by reading the code only: deps/xenon-pthread stacks are `aligned(128)`, and the top is `+256K−0x100`.
+- MSR[VEC] on all threads (startup_from_xell.S) was checked by reading the code only.
+- The exception handler not saving VRs was checked by reading the code only. libxenon.a has only 2 vector instructions (`vxor`/`stvx` v0, in `kv_read`), so its interrupt code does not clobber VRs.
+- qemu used `-mcpu=7450` scalar code, not `-mcpu=cell -mpowerpc64`. Only the vector semantics and the shared C code were tested.
